Hi Igor,

SIZE_E_1 stays at 0 and the diff stays as it is. Your reason is better than the
one I would have given: a register built from the trace with one field swapped
to upstream's value is a configuration nothing has ever run, and that is worth
more than a smaller diff.

Your question has an answer and it is partly uncomfortable, so let me give the
measurement rather than the summary. Since we last spoke I swept SIZE_E_2 across
all eight of its values on a second path, the int4 one, at 64 output channels:

    SIZE_E_2       0    1    2    3    4..7
    words written  32   32   36   40   the job hangs

and the count that came out CORRECT stayed at 32 in all of them, so what the
field moves is how many words the block writes, not how many channels it
computes.

So on that path the field is additive in FOURS, not in sixteens. That does not
sit well with the semantics I sent you on the 17th, where SIZE_E_n + 1 counted
16-channel atoms. It is a different register value on a different precision, so
it does not refute the reading for the path it was read from, and I am not going
to pretend it supports it either.

SIZE_E_1 on that path is inert: setting it from 1 to 0 leaves the channel count
at 40 and the probe's sixteen point window unchanged. That is a smaller claim
than "no effect" and it is the size of what I actually read. SIZE_E_0 is inert at
0 and hangs at 2 and at 6. RGP_CNTER and OD_BYPASS are inert, RESERVED_0 hangs.

Now the part your question actually asks. I said I would cross the two and I
have, at seven channel counts, 16 through 64 in steps of 8, with SIZE_E_1 forced
to 0 against its traced 1:

    N               16   24   32   40   48   56   64
    words written   16   24   32   40   48   56   64
    correct         16   16   32   24   48   32   64

Read the four that are multiples of sixteen: 16, 32, 48 and 64 come back
identical to the traced value, so SIZE_E_1 is inert there and your conservative
choice costs nothing at those shapes.

The other three I cannot attribute, and that is my fault rather than a result:
the round ran 24, 40 and 56 only with SIZE_E_1 at 0 and never at 1, so there is
no same-round control for them. The shortfall at those N looks like the same
sixteen-channel quantum below, which would make it nothing to do with SIZE_E_1,
but I am not going to hand you a reading with no control under it. If it matters
to you I will run the missing half.

So your conservative choice and the honest one are still the same choice: inert
where I can prove it, unproven where I cannot.

And there is a loose end in that table which I would rather hand you than tidy
away. An earlier round, with 0x4050 at its traced value, measured that when the
channel register asks for exactly n the block writes floor(n/16)*16 of them --
24 asks and 16 arrive. The table above, at SIZE_E_1 = 0, writes all 24. Those
are two different rounds and I will not put weight on a cross-round comparison,
but if it survives a same-round control then SIZE_E_1 is not inert at the N that
are not multiples of sixteen, and it is the write count that it moves. That is
the opposite of what I have been telling you, so it goes on the board before it
goes in a mail: the missing half is N of 24, 40 and 56 at the traced value, and
it is one entry.

The re-run offer is noted and I will take it when v9 goes. Glad the trip was
fine, and I hope the countryside is treating the board well.

Regards,
Jiaxing
